  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Elevation dependent wet snowfall in the Northern\nTier and Endless Mountains overnight through Friday morning \n\nIn the wake of circulation rotating across south central PA this\nafternoon, intensifying cold air advection and increasing FGEN\nwill eventually result in dynamic column cooling and lowering \nsnow levels across north central and northeast PA tonight. Snow\ntotals trended higher for this cycle especially above 1500ft\nelevation given colder model trends and increasing banding\npotential. Despite marginal temperatures, confidence has\nincreased for up to 2 inches of wet snow across the northern \ntier/Endless Mtns overnight into early Friday. Locally higher\namounts to 4 inches are possible especially within a comma head\nbanded snow feature. This feature could also drag snow levels \ncloser to the ground at lower elevations. The bulk of the \naccumulation may be confined to non paved surfaces which may \nlimit winter driving impacts. Snowfall could extend farther to \nthe south impacting higher terrain from Northern Clinton to \nSchuylkill Counties. Odds for accumulation decrease during the \ndaytime hours Friday. \n\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Breezy and colder weekend with rain and snow \nshowers downwind of Lake Erie \n\nBreezy and colder conditions will grip the area this weekend. \nWind gusts in the 25-35 mph range are likely producing wind\nmin wind chills in the 15-30F range. The lake effect and \nupslope snow is expected to produce a coating to one inch of \nsnow across the Laurels and the northwest mountains with \nlocalized amounts pushing closer to two inches in the favored\nsnowbelts and summits. A mix of rain/snow showers are possible\nat times between the I-99 and I-81 corridors. Rain/snow showers\ntaper off by Sunday night. \n\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Dry and seasonal pattern returns next week \n\nAll indication point to another stretch of dry, tranquil, and\nseasonal weather returning for next week 11/16-11/20.\nTemperatures will moderate and trend warmer with fcst max temps\nclimbing 10 to 15 degrees above the historical average by late\nweek.",
